## Code style
- Formatting enforced by ktlint (kotlinter: ignoreFormatFailures = false, ignoreLintFailures = false,
  reporters checkstyle + plain); rules in .editorconfig; run `make format` before committing.
- Naming: classes/DTOs PascalCase; functions/properties camelCase; tests use backtick names;
  routes registered as extension functions on Route; names describe what things do.
- Serialization: @Serializable on DTOs; explicit @SerialName("snake_case") on external payloads;
  reuse serializers under serializer/ (UUIDSerializer, LocalDateSerializer, OffsetDateTimeSerializer);
  every DTO implements the Dto interface.
- Logging: io.github.oshai.kotlinlogging.KLogger everywhere; prod logs JSON, local logs text,
  both via src/main/resources/logback.xml; flowId propagated into MDC.
- Errors: use the Errors hierarchy in result/Errors.kt (Errors.ValidationError, Errors.NotFoundError,
  or a specific service error), never ad hoc RuntimeException; StatusPages turns them into
  RFC 7807 problem responses.
  Example: `repository.findById(id) ?: throw Errors.NotFoundError("Transport", id.toString())`
- Database: Exposed SQL DSL (not DAO); transactions via transaction(database) { ... } in repository
  methods; always pass the wired Database into repository constructors; Flyway is the source of truth
  for schema; raw SQL only when the DSL is impossible or much less convenient.

## General rules
- Immutable objects by default; functions do not mutate state unless clearly justified.
- Every new logic/feature is covered by tests.
- Three layers API → Domain → Data access, one direction only; contracts via interfaces.
- No magic numbers (named constants); avoid deep nesting; ~120-char line limit.
- Follow existing structure and patterns.
- No default values on model/DTO properties (causes bugs in layer transformations).

## Surgical changes
- Touch only what you must; clean up only your own mess.
- Don't "improve" adjacent code, comments or formatting; don't refactor what isn't broken.
- Match existing style. Mention unrelated dead code, don't delete it.
- Remove imports/variables/functions your own change made unused.
- Every changed line should trace to the request.

## Simplicity first
- Minimum code that solves the problem. No speculative features, no single-use abstractions,
  no unrequested configurability, no error handling for impossible cases.
- If 200 lines could be 50, rewrite. Would a senior engineer call it overcomplicated? Then simplify.